Founded in 1889 and listed since 1894, Rheinmetall AG is a Düsseldorf‑headquartered, DAX‑component (since 20 Mar 2023) integrated technology group with five divisions (Vehicle Systems Europe & International, Weapon & Ammunition, Electronic Solutions, Power Systems), reporting FY 2024: €9.8 bn sales, €1,478 m operating result (15.2% margin), €55.0 bn order backlog and 31,000+ employees across 171 sites.
As a leading defence systems supplier, Rheinmetall’s portfolio spans tracked & wheeled vehicles (Panther KF51 MBT, RMMV HX2 logistics trucks, Fuchs Evolution 6×6 APC, Caracal 4×4 air‑assault vehicle), artillery (wheeled self‑propelled howitzer), munitions (Expal acquisition €1.2 bn, new 20–35 mm ammo plant Unterlüß from 2025), loitering munitions (HERO), UAS (LUNA NG), CBRN reconnaissance and digital/teleoperation solutions.
In civilian markets, Power Systems and Automotive Technology divisions supply pumps, pistons, plain bearings, aluminum blocks, electric motors, thermal & air management, balance‑of‑plant for fuel‑cells, hydrogen storage & transport systems, lightweight composites, and connectivity & ADAS (in‑cabin sensing), enabling ICE, BEV, FCEV and autonomous mobility.
In 2025 alone, Rheinmetall executed key M&A and partnerships: acquired Stascheit GmbH (14 Apr 2025) and Hagedorn‑NC GmbH (7 Apr 2025); established MGCS Project Company GmbH in Cologne (16 Apr 2025); launched joint Centre of Excellence with Lockheed Martin for European security (29 Apr 2025); and achieved a hydrogen storage/transport milestone (27 Apr 2025) alongside expanded Australian maintenance capabilities (28 Apr 2025).
Under CEO Armin Papperger (since 1 Jan 2013) and Supervisory Board Chairman Ulrich Grillo, Rheinmetall AG as parent holding defines group strategy, governance, M&A and risk management—anchored by an ambitious sustainability agenda targeting CO2 neutrality by 2035 and integrated CSR.
