Rheinmetall AG committed to group‑wide climate neutrality by 2035 and installed 1 635 photovoltaic modules on roofs of its Pierburg SA plant in Abadiño, Spain (Nov. 2022), targeting 730 000 kWh/year of solar generation and reducing 175 tCO2/year—an indicator of shifting capex toward renewables in its Power Systems (20.8% of sales) segment.
Since its first energy audit in 2009, Pierburg SA has deployed systematic energy‑efficiency capex (geothermal exchange, pipe insulation, automated fan‑heater/compressed‑air leak‑detection controls, LED/KNX lighting, coolant filtration/briquetting), demonstrating scaling of low‑carbon investments within Rheinmetall’s Electronic Solutions (13.5% of sales) operations.
Pierburg SA’s sustainability measures earned recognition from the Energy Department of the Basque Government (2014), EFQM for energy‑management good practice (2015) and CEBEC (Province of Bizkaia, 2016), underscoring that targeted capex in environmental upgrades yields regulatory and reputational ROI.
Power Systems (actuators, EGR systems, throttle valves, exhaust dampers) and Electronic Solutions (sensor/networking, cyberspace protection, radars, drones) together generate ~34.3% of Rheinmetall’s revenues; aligning these core products with the CO2 neutrality goal implies increased R&D/capex in electrification and digital energy‑management technologies.
Rheinmetall’s net sales geographic mix—Germany 30.4%, Europe 46.6%, Asia & Middle East 9.6%, Americas 7.6%—and 28 539 employees suggest primary capex for decarbonization will focus on European production sites (e.g. Spain), where scope 1/2 emissions are most material.
The 2035 roadmap necessitates a reallocation of capex from legacy defense segments (vehicle systems 38.7%, weapons/ammo 26%) toward sustainable energy infrastructure and low‑carbon product innovation in Power Systems and Electronic Solutions, signaling a strategic pivot in investment priorities.
Rheinmetall’s Werk Niedersachsen at Unterlüß (Lüneburg Heath, Lower Saxony) was inaugurated with a groundbreaking ceremony on 11 Feb 2024 attended by Chancellor Olaf Scholz, Danish PM Mette Frederiksen, Defence Minister Boris Pistorius and CEO Armin Papperger, marking a €300 million company‑financed capex.
Werk Niedersachsen has a modular, scalable plan to produce 50,000 artillery shells in year 1, 100,000 in year 2 and ramp to 200,000 shells / year (plus up to 1,900 tonnes of RDX explosive and optional rocket engines/warheads) by year 3, achieving 50 % domestic value added in year 1, 80 % in year 2 and 100 % by year 3.
A new medium‑calibre (20–35 mm) ammunition line at Unterlüß was commissioned in summer 2023 “at great expense” to satisfy surging global demand for automatic‑cannon and anti‑aircraft rounds on systems including Marder, Gepard and Puma.
In Feb 2023, the German MoD (on behalf of Ukraine) placed a low‑three‑digit‑million € order for 300,000 rounds of 35 mm Gepard ammo (150,000 APDS‑T, 150,000 HEI‑T); APDS‑T deliveries began summer 2023, with production shifting to HEI‑T by mid‑2024.
In Dec 2024, Ukraine’s MoD commissioned a further 180,000 rounds of 35 mm HEI‑T Gepard ammunition (order valued in the high double‑digit million euro range), with deliveries scheduled to start in 2026, funded by the German government.
Rheinmetall secured a €200 million+ order in Apr 2023 from a European customer for 20–35 mm medium‑calibre ammunition (for IFVs and air defence) to be shipped between 2023 and 2025, reflecting Unterlüß’s role as the sole single‑source supplier for these calibres.
