Rheinmetall AG’s Compliance Risk Assessment is governed by four prevention objectives—legal/organizational oversight, financial risk avoidance, reputation protection and operational efficiency of Group‑wide Compliance structures—and feeds directly into continuous improvements of the Compliance Management System across headquarters and all subsidiaries in Germany and abroad.
Group‑wide compliance risk assessments comprise a top‑down analysis to flag higher‑risk business segments (e.g., defence/security) and a bottom‑up subsidiary‑level gross‑net risk comparison, with findings integrated into remediation measures at every Group level.
In the defence and security business, Rheinmetall enforces stringent anti‑corruption and anti‑bribery rules; by contrast its automotive segment places special emphasis on antitrust, anti‑money laundering and supply‑chain risk management, reflecting distinct regulatory exposures.
Rheinmetall’s standardized risk management system—mandated by the Executive Board of Rheinmetall AG—uses the three lines of defence model: 1st line (operational management), 2nd line (risk and compliance management plus internal controls) and 3rd line (Internal Audit) as independent overseer.
An annual risk inventory review during corporate planning catalogs material risks (with probabilities of occurrence, damage potential, early‑warning indicators, responsibilities and countermeasures), while operating units and central functions submit monthly standardized reports to Group Controlling and the Executive Board.
Unexpected material risks or adverse significant developments are escalated to the Executive Board on an ad hoc basis, ensuring dynamic oversight and prompt introduction of additional mitigation measures when required.